.TH FL-FILES 1 "November 2026" "fldesktop 0.1.0" "fldesktop"
.SH NAME
fl-files \- file manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfl-files\fR [\fB\-\-connect\fR] [\fIFOLDER\fR|\fBtrash:///\fR|\fBnetwork:///\fR|\fILOCATION\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Browse and manage files: thumbnails, copy/move in the background, the XDG trash, open with,
properties and permissions, bookmarks, a folder tree and file search.
.SS Views
\fBIcons\fR (Ctrl+1), \fBCompact\fR (Ctrl+2, small icons in columns) and \fBDetails\fR (Ctrl+3, a
table). Ctrl+plus/minus, Ctrl+mouse wheel and Ctrl+0 change the icon size. In the Details view,
click a column header to sort, drag a header border to resize a column, and right-click the
header to choose columns: Size, Type, Modified, Permissions and Owner. By default each folder
remembers its own view, sort order and icon size.
.PP
F9 shows a folder tree instead of the places list. Ctrl+D bookmarks the current folder;
right-click a bookmark to rename or remove it (bookmarks are shared with GTK applications).
.SS Tabs
Ctrl+T opens a tab at the current folder, Ctrl+W closes it (and the window with the last tab).
Ctrl+Tab and Ctrl+Shift+Tab (or Ctrl+Page Down/Up) go to the next and previous tab, Alt+1 to Alt+9
to a tab by its place. A middle click on a folder, a place or a bookmark opens it in a new tab
behind the current one (or in a new window, see the preferences). Each tab has its own folder,
history, view and search. Tabs can be dragged to another place, or out of the strip into a
window of their own; right-click a tab to duplicate it or to close other tabs. The tab bar shows
with two or more tabs, or always.
.SS Split view
F4 (or \fBSplit View\fR in the menus) shows two tabs side by side: the current one and the
next, or a new tab at the same folder. A click in a pane makes it the current one (a line at its
top marks it); the toolbar, the location and the keys work on it. The context menu copies or
moves the selection to the other pane's folder, and files can be dragged between the panes.
F4 again shows one pane; closing either tab does too.
.SS Renaming many files
With more than one item selected, F2 (or \fBRename\fR) opens a dialog that renames them
together. The name pattern takes \fB{name}\fR (the old name without its extension),
\fB{ext}\fR (the extension with its dot), \fB{n}\fR (a number, counting from a chosen
start) and \fB{n:3}\fR (a number with three digits); then a text (or a regular expression,
with \fB$1\fR in the replacement) is replaced and the letter case changed. A preview shows the
new names; names used twice or taken by another file stop the rename. Names may swap.
.SS Properties and the administrator
The Properties dialog (Alt+Enter) shows the type, size, dates and owner. For one's own files
it changes the permissions and the group (to one the user is in); for a folder, \fBApply to
everything inside\fR gives the same read and write permissions to all files and folders inside
it, and execute to the folders.
\fBOpen as Administrator\fR (context menu) opens a folder in a new fl-files, or a file in
\fBfl-edit\fR(1), as root through \fBpkexec\fR(1); that window's title says so.
.SS Network locations
With GVfs (\fBgvfs\fR, \fBgvfs-fuse\fR and the backends such as \fBgvfs-smb\fR, \fBgvfs-mtp\fR)
fl-files opens network shares, phones and cameras. Type a location into the location field
(\fBsmb://server/share\fR, \fBsftp://user@host/path\fR, \fBftp://host\fR,
\fBdavs://host/path\fR, \fBnfs://host/export\fR), give it on the command line, or use
\fBConnect to Server\fR (in the menus; \fB\-\-connect\fR opens it at start), which keeps recent
servers and can bookmark them. A password is asked for when the server wants one, and can be
remembered for the session or in the keyring. The sidebar lists \fBNetwork\fR (the computers
and shares found nearby), every connected location with a button to disconnect it, phones and
cameras to mount, and bookmarks of network locations.
.PP
The files are reached through gvfs-fuse (\fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gvfs\fR), so they can be copied,
moved, opened and searched like local ones; the location field shows the location rather than
that folder. Network locations have no trash: deleting asks to delete for good. Thumbnails of
remote files are off unless the preferences turn them on, and their folders are not watched for
changes (F5 reloads).
.SS Finding files
Ctrl+Shift+F (or F3, or the search button) opens the find bar. It searches the current folder and
its subfolders by name (a part of the name, or a pattern with \fB*\fR and \fB?\fR), by text inside
files, by type (files or folders), by modification date and by size; hidden items optionally.
The \fB...\fR menu adds case-sensitive matching, regular expressions and following symbolic
links. Results appear as they are found and can be opened, copied, renamed and deleted like
any other files; the context menu offers \fBOpen Containing Folder\fR. Escape stops a running
search, then closes the find bar.
.SS Keys
Alt+arrows navigate, Ctrl+L edits the location, Ctrl+F filters the current folder, Ctrl+H toggles
hidden files, F2 renames (one item, or many: see above), F4 splits the view, Delete moves to the trash, Shift+Delete deletes, F5 reloads,
Ctrl+M shows or hides the menu bar (File, Edit, View, Go, Bookmarks, Help; hidden by default,
the menu button has the same commands), Ctrl+comma opens the preferences.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI~/.config/fldesktop/files.ini\fR
Preferences: default view, date format, single-click opening, trash confirmation, thumbnails,
columns, tabs, the menu bar.
.TP
\fI~/.config/fldesktop/files-folders.ini\fR
Remembered views of individual folders.
.TP
\fI~/.config/gtk-3.0/bookmarks\fR
Bookmarks.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBfl-wm\fR(1), \fBfl-term\fR(1), \fBfl-media-player\fR(1)
